; The same unsoundness one level in: a type variable in a FUNCTION-TYPED
; parameter, where the callee still chooses the type.
;
; `347-result-only-tyvar.ax` has the shape everyone sees first - the
; variable is the RESULT, and the rule that catches it asks whether any
; parameter mentions it. That question has a wrong answer here.
; `demand`'s `a` is inside a parameter, so a rule reading sides files it
; under "witnessed" and reports nothing at all. Measured on the compiler
; before this fixture existed: `check` answered OK and
;
;     (demand strLen)
;
; exited 139 - 42 handed to `strLen` as a String pointer, the same
; dereference `conjure` reaches by the obvious route.
;
; What separates them is VARIANCE, not side. A parameter is a position
; the CALLER fills; the left of an arrow INSIDE that parameter flips
; back, and is a position the CALLEE fills. `a` is one of those: to call
; `f` at all, `demand` must produce an `a`, and nothing the caller hands
; over says what one is. There are the same two ways to make one - a
; `cast` or a call to something that casts - and no third.
;
; THE DIVERGING READING IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE, which is why this arm has
; no fixpoint behind it. `(:: panic (-> String a))` is honest because a
; function that never returns never produced the `a` it promised.
; `demand` returns an `Int` perfectly well; it fabricates on the way,
; and a body that also looped forever would fabricate just the same.
;
; `witnessed` is the control that matters most, because it is the shape
; ordinary higher-order code is written in. `b` appears on the RIGHT of
; the callback's arrow - the caller's function produces it - and `a` on
; its left is also the second parameter, which the caller supplies. Both
; are witnessed, both must stay silent, and a rule that reported either
; would refuse `map`.
;
; `alsoResult` is the shape the two arms could double-report: `a` is
; inside the callback AND is the result. It draws exactly one
; diagnostic, from the returned-variable arm, because that arm is the
; one the divergence fixpoint can still answer.
;
; `divDemand` is that same overlap with the fixpoint's answer flipped,
; and it is why the arms DECIDE the overlap rather than subtract it.
; Its result variable is excused - it never returns, so `for all a` is
; the honest type - but it hands `f` a fabricated `a` on the way to not
; returning, and a divergence that happens afterwards does not unmake
; the call. The first version of this analysis subtracted the result's
; variables from the demanded set, which reads as "the other arm has
; it"; the other arm was silent, and this checked OK and exited 139.
; Measured 2026-08-25, and it is the second 139 in this file.
(:: demand (-> (-> a Int) Int))

(fn (demand f)
  (f
    (cast a 42)
  )
)

(:: witnessed (-> (-> a b) a b))

(fn (witnessed f x) (f x))

;@axiom:raw
(:: declared (-> (-> a Int) Int))

(fn (declared f)
  (f
    (cast a 42)
  )
)

(:: alsoResult (-> (-> a Int) a))

(fn (alsoResult f)
  (cast a 42)
)

(:: divDemand (-> (-> a Int) a))

(fn (divDemand f)
  {
    (f
      (cast a 42)
    )
    (let ((mut i 0))
      {
        (while true
          (set i (+ i 1)))
        (cast a i)
      }
    )
  }
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
